#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630a6635-aac0-5627-82c8-8072b74dd8f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "02afcfcd-c405-5add-a291-75e6db923e3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13943" :question/problem "What is the intersection of the intervals shown below, expressed as a number line?\n\n![](images/e2/e2da9ff989c1cee3365f88b4ba7bc8dba9ef31f20dc49ae8e4f87a79243473b0.png)" :question/worked-solution "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/43/4333901508bff833b854769feabb729b836cdb3aae9827b5170546b2ffbe7d35.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](images/f9/f9d3a9e1da4be61938e41eb5ebe308ed85cd1f60f4fd29f211b2f72071afe159.png)\n\n**Watch out!** The point $-1$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c60e9f3-3322-57c7-85da-e65ea232ef9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "669f96c6-1505-5b02-88aa-c99af18b24d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13950" :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty).$ Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We can start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty, 3]$ and $(0, \\infty)$ separately:\n\n![](images/25/255da904e7d271006cef6a272379b8f160fc6cc9e5f8ede39d91141aea2eff7b.png)\n\n![](images/31/31ad28b45aa2cefa4d83cc06710e9ae81446809ec0ac42da5baae36e235e35a9.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/6a/6a92713b9964c9b1583622553a7cdb3c9c700ecad95cc0581f1538d18d1fa42a.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty, 3]\\cap(0, \\infty)={\\color{blue}(0,3]}.$ This corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 < x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval. On the other hand, the point $3$ is included in the intersection because it lies in both intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85687901-9285-5cd2-a5f2-24299bd7d3d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given as Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "5aa36af9-33b3-58a4-b1ce-297f170faa44" :question/math-academy-id "e-13954" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x < 3$ and $x \\leq -1$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Three Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "db91e510-bd32-5272-be39-c5015758c81b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13958" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x < 1,$  $x \\geq  0,$ and $-2 < x < 4,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630a6635-aac0-5627-82c8-8072b74dd8f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464642a6-73d9-53ff-bfb3-5f251c2d5b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/202e1967e40a092a2c74791030a9ebb738fbf85c1732f51016530399c497fa06.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505248} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad8ea7b-1cf2-5439-90d3-243dae0ad935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae2ea8641bd37c2b7c906e5cff4872bba786fd55977095cd167af4beda2974bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b92a4db-bda7-5a58-8239-0115074f3cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17d75bd72023fd159d76adc4ec9618144c3a596ed5ae245e4e391d1ead36a80f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505257} {:answer/id #uuid "a47c27ff-b988-576f-86d6-9628c6a5eb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/66cdd62f55cb053e3e0f61a45c11b44ab0e53aa2a5732dc66772cb32471c9151.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505431} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f3ddf8-ec18-5ba6-ad50-51dc55beceba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43909690ac4de9222d8d9a26c4118a39bc76521f15d3d49e9248ff30a25e3b54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a47c27ff-b988-576f-86d6-9628c6a5eb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/66cdd62f55cb053e3e0f61a45c11b44ab0e53aa2a5732dc66772cb32471c9151.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505431} :answer-field/id #uuid "d584ed97-b993-5dd4-b725-3d7a3f4ffff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9960d442-4263-5f59-861d-9bea9c4351f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101831" :question/problem "![](images/be/becff2a10906d3b119081b0ccd41093cdbbff7fee90c4ed440032bc46790bad1.png)\n\nWhat is the intersection of the intervals shown above, expressed  as a number line?" :question/worked-solution "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/94/9428f89e1751a7e91f5a869e1549af8525c9989a8c743a1acfff00430c879bae.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](images/66/66cdd62f55cb053e3e0f61a45c11b44ab0e53aa2a5732dc66772cb32471c9151.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c60e9f3-3322-57c7-85da-e65ea232ef9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e3c5da-5865-567e-a5d3-a56e2ffe211f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x<8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505230} {:answer/id #uuid "9e97b3da-ea49-5728-95d7-76098f4e03a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505416} {:answer/id #uuid "a17bc6c6-bbd0-5690-826b-8ef886e34033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<x<8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505425} {:answer/id #uuid "dadf839a-b742-583a-9087-bffd99709c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4<x<8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505524} {:answer/id #uuid "db36fe97-68ba-5c44-9ec2-fa9e16dc383c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dadf839a-b742-583a-9087-bffd99709c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4<x<8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505524} :answer-field/id #uuid "141c353f-f862-5687-8396-3900d4d223ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17502c35-e040-5657-8233-e531c12d0255" :question/math-academy-id "q-101840" :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $[1,8)\\cap (4,8].$ Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the two intervals $[1,8)$ and $(4,8]$ separately:\n\n![](images/63/63a236a899eff365f7964a182690470011cbee2f393586fb3a673e5332b7346e.png)\n\n![](images/9b/9b2db1951a30f71c8635e0daec81ae6931e28c2c44489952c65e10d4c95ea549.png)\n\nThe intersection $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f5/f59e4645230b48b89fb445fd3cb586516361848418e2d287e6d208c97d9dcc00.png)\n\nThus, $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]=(4,8).$ This corresponds to the inequality $4<x<8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85687901-9285-5cd2-a5f2-24299bd7d3d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals Given as Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca3d33e-aae9-5c84-8695-fff65c899896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505154} {:answer/id #uuid "27fea126-1ba8-5e27-b001-17cb104d1eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505175} {:answer/id #uuid "34b04d05-b9ac-56ed-b620-2a3f832647fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505198} {:answer/id #uuid "42c57f21-ca0a-5db9-aeb8-56447070178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505238} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9a4cd8-b78c-5941-bb37-82661ed67dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca3d33e-aae9-5c84-8695-fff65c899896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505154} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e701a6d-6b18-569a-8eea-54880c65a3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f46011a6-2365-5214-8b00-ddd367e7ff91" :question/math-academy-id "q-102006" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $3<x<7$ and $2\\le x\\le 5$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/46/468dea33da7365430c2e08bcbf13d176fdafabf64aaac0a940a01c108b1b0e64.png)\n\n![](images/41/413c2bbe7a61c8983ea92ff4bb73bba27fc05856eae5f1fdb9614aa2b50f92aa.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/51/51a4f2d24dd17ae11e1530b6765782163b9c8f79b69509b458037e747ae0dd37.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(3,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c60e9f3-3322-57c7-85da-e65ea232ef9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Two Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505232} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab51b64-d5ad-523e-aa8e-5100a33e1f3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505493} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8be00fb-1abe-5071-b61a-57d715646d2a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505115} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8eb0997-7805-5dd6-a59a-d474cc7a0565" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae04db08-eab8-56dc-9a49-71b8878683c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-214597" :question/problem "Express the intersection of intervals $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)$ as an inequality.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-1,2)$ and $[0,3)$ separately:\n\n![](images/a5/a5a6b966c5d98863cfed20ccb13f3a1ca5413887f228f091d504bb7cd0001b37.png)\n\n![](images/82/823bf1d62aadc91ac15e52260a9919622358144865f0522f34389d48f8c11273.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/9c/9c7ea77f9699601b97ff38a886af6418b44fa9568009aaa4b36be17efa03cbee.png)\n\nThus, $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)=[0,2).$ This corresponds to the inequality $0\\le x<2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Three Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10753e46-1efe-5e3a-9b77-601c98c10d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505131} {:answer/id #uuid "28450f25-46fa-5cbe-893b-dfe5cf85579a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505176} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ca0c04-f822-57d6-b94e-709cea01f963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505430} {:answer/id #uuid "baea9cb8-94f7-5837-a1f2-7de40fff18c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505466} {:answer/id #uuid "d502482d-a9dc-5977-b331-a98d3ca585ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d502482d-a9dc-5977-b331-a98d3ca585ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505517} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae653d46-ca6d-5a93-abdb-d21333d9e21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcd15d2f-86ee-5a4a-83a6-5df06fb72031" :question/math-academy-id "q-25894" :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x\\ge -6,$ $-3<x<5,$ and  $x\\le 3,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/d6/d68477510b8339f48eebe5105dd081661e0c299a89712662b6f13a7e15c7b2e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(-3,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d916584b-c94e-557f-a438-f0464c1cea87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of Three Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333d24